From: Stained Glass OS <ke7oxh@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] winex11: the keys of a layout have its Windows layout's virtual keys (9152)

On Windows every layout's kbd*.dll names the keys of the main block: a key
gets the letter it types, and the other character keys get the virtual
keys that layout gives them -- kbdsw's O-umlaut key is VK_OEM_3 and its +
key VK_OEM_PLUS, kbduk's # key VK_OEM_7, kbd106's @ key VK_OEM_3, kbdsp's
n-tilde key VK_OEM_3. Programs read those: games and emulators bind the
console and menu keys by virtual key, AutoHotkey hotkeys name vkC0 and the
like, terminal emulators and Office map their shortcuts by them. Only
French and German were right, and only for a layout loaded beside the X
map: the X map's own layout took Wine's position tables (US keys for
Spanish, Italian, Norwegian, Danish, Finnish, UK, Swiss, Portuguese,
Dutch, Belgian, Turkish ...), and every other loaded layout kept the US
key at each place.

Now one table, from the layouts' published virtual-key tables
(kbdlayout.info), gives each Windows layout's main-block keys where they
differ from kbdus, by XKB layout (and variant: Canadian Multilingual,
Polish 214, Turkish F, Japanese 106 ...). The tables of a layout other
than the X map's are built on it, and the X map's own character keys take
it by scan code (letters keep their letter, keys X gives no character keep
theirs). A layout variant of its own (Dvorak, Colemak, BEPO) does not take
its layout's.

Gate test/kbdvkeys-gate.sh (Xvfb, setxkbmap): per layout family, the X
map's and the loaded layout's MapVirtualKeyEx scan -> VK for the keys
Windows moves. Mutant SG_MUTANT_KBD_US_OEM.
